---
// /methodology/ — SEO_ASTRO_PLAN.md §11 and §13.
//
// This page is not optional and it is not marketing. Every superlative on the site — "best",
// a 4.6/5, a league-table rank — is a comparative claim under IRDAI advertising rules, and
// §13 lists unsourced comparative claims as a live compliance risk. The mitigation named
// there is that every score resolves to this page. So every plan page links here, and this
// page has to actually explain the arithmetic rather than gesture at it.
//
// The weights are read from src/data/methodology.json, the same file the scoring uses. If
// they are ever recalculated, this page changes with them — there is no second copy to drift.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Disclaimer from '../components/seo/Disclaimer.astro'
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema'
import { fitDescription } from '../lib/seo'
import { CATEGORY_LABELS } from '../lib/plans'
import methodology from '../data/methodology.json'
import meta from '../data/meta.json'

const planWeights = methodology.plan
const companyWeights = methodology.company as Record<string, Array<{ label: string; weight: number; note: string }>>

const trail = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'How we score plans', path: '/methodology/' },
]

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Are Myinsurancebro scores paid for or influenced by insurers?',
    a:
      'No. Scores are calculated from the published data described on this page. Insurers cannot ' +
      'pay to raise a score, to be included, or to have a weakness removed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where do the claim settlement ratios come from?',
    a:
      `From IRDAI's Annual Report and public grievance disclosures, as ${meta.period.toLowerCase()}. ` +
      'A single year moves too much on small claim volumes to be a fair signal, which is why we ' +
      'average three.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are the premiums on this site quotes?',
    a:
      'No. Every premium shown is an illustration for a named profile — a stated age, cover ' +
      'amount and city. Your own premium depends on your age, health, habits and underwriting, ' +
      'and only the insurer can quote it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How often are the figures updated?',
    a:
      `Each plan record carries its own last-verified date, shown on the plan page. The current ` +
      `dataset was verified in ${meta.updated} against IRDAI disclosures and insurer policy wordings.`,
  },
]

const description = fitDescription(
  'How Myinsurancebro scores insurance plans: plan features 45%, insurer strength 45%, premium 10%, ' +
    'from IRDAI disclosures.',
  ['No insurer can pay to change a score.', 'Independent and unpaid.'],
)
---

<Base
  title="How We Score Insurance Plans"
  description={description}
  current="methodology"
  keywords={[
    'insurance plan scoring methodology',
    'how insurance plans are rated India',
    'claim settlement ratio source',
  ]}
  schemas={[breadcrumbSchema(trail), faqSchema(faqs)]}
>
  <article class="plan-page">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><span aria-current="page">How we score plans</span></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h1>How we score insurance plans</h1>
    <p class="plan-subtitle">
      Every score on this site resolves to the arithmetic on this page.
    </p>

    <p class="answer-block">
      Plans are scored out of 5 on three weighted components: plan features 45%, insurer strength
      45% and premium 10%. Insurer inputs come from IRDAI's Annual Report and public grievance
      disclosures, {meta.period.toLowerCase()}. Plan inputs come from the insurer's own policy
      wording. No insurer pays to be listed, ranked or scored.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What goes into a plan score</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>
            Component weights for a plan score. The same weights apply in every category.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Component</th>
              <th scope="col">Weight</th>
              <th scope="col">What it measures</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {planWeights.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                <td>{row.weight}%</td>
                <td class="cell-prose">{row.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Features and insurer strength are weighted equally and deliberately. A generous plan from
        an insurer that argues over claims is not a good outcome, and neither is a reliable
        insurer's thin product. Premium carries only 10% — price matters, but the cheapest policy
        that does not pay is the most expensive thing on this page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What goes into an insurer score</h2>
      <p>
        Insurers are scored separately from plans, and the weights differ by category because the
        thing that goes wrong differs. A term claim is one large binary payout years after the
        policy was sold; a health claim is a stream of smaller ones with a hospital waiting.
      </p>

      {Object.entries(companyWeights).map(([category, rows]) => (
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Insurer scoring for {CATEGORY_LABELS[category as keyof typeof CATEGORY_LABELS].toLowerCase()}.
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Component</th>
                <th scope="col">Weight</th>
                <th scope="col">What it measures</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {rows.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                  <td>{row.weight}%</td>
                  <td class="cell-prose">{row.note}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <dl class="key-facts">
        <div><dt>Claim settlement &amp; complaints</dt><dd>IRDAI Annual Report</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Averaging period</dt><dd>{meta.period}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Cover, exclusions, waiting periods</dt><dd>Insurer policy wording</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Last verified</dt><dd>{meta.updated}</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Every plan page lists the specific documents its figures came from, with the clause where
        a number is contested, and its own last-verified date. Where we could not verify a figure
        against a primary source, the plan is not published.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How we stay independent</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Insurers cannot pay for inclusion, for a higher score, or to remove a weakness.</li>
        <li>
          Every plan page names what the plan does <em>not</em> cover, and what we do not like
          about it. A plan with no listed weaknesses has not been reviewed properly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Myinsurancebro is an IRDAI-licensed advisor and earns commission on policies placed
          through us. That is disclosed rather than hidden — and it is why the scores are computed
          from published data instead of from judgement we would have an interest in.
        </li>
        <li>Premiums are illustrations for stated profiles. We do not present them as quotes.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a score does not tell you</h2>
      <p>
        A score ranks plans against each other on the evidence available. It cannot know your
        health history, your dependants, your city's hospital costs or which exclusion will
        matter to you in ten years. Use it to shortlist, then read the policy wording — and
        talk to an advisor before you buy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((faq) => (
        <details>
          <summary><h3>{faq.q}</h3></summary>
          <p>{faq.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </section>

    <Disclaimer />
  </article>
</Base>
